    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Hadahid fault is 10 km-long normal fault which was formed during the Miocene rifting of the Gulf of Suez area. The fault consists of three major left-stepping segments, each with a different offset profile, and many minor segments. Shale from two Cretaceous formations, 35 and 50 m thick, is smeared along the fault, occasionally with sandstone enclaves among the shale units. The thickness of shale entrapped along the fault systematically decreases with increasing fault offset. The shale smear thickness becomes zero at offsets of approximately 150 and 230 m corresponding to the 35 and 50 m shale layers respectively.\r\n\r\nThe Hadahid fault is segmented along-strike on a km-scale due to the decrease of displacement along the fault. Its central segment is broken down into a series of left-stepping en echelon faults due to the interference of shale within the fault zone with fault propagation. The central and northern segments of the Hadahid fault overlap for approximately 600 m then coalesce further north. The coalesced fault offset (max. 140 m) is divided on the two faults segments and, consequently, is accompanied by an increase in the thickness of the shale smear on overlapping faults.\r\n\r\nOn even a smaller scale, faults in the Hadahid block show along-dip segmentation because the incipient fault segments initially form in the stiffer beds with an en echelon pattern that has a step sense towards-the-hanging-wall. The amount of offset between the en echelon segments is related to the thickness of the shale layer. As fault offset increases, the fault segments do not penetrate the shale layers and hence allow the shale to \"bend\" between the two fault segments. As the displacement increases, the incipient fault segments coalesce to form a master fault.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
